tly two different moves are played.\n3. We should continue to vary our moves more frequently to avoid predictable patterns and to confuse Player C.\n4. Message: Let's try Paper this round and see how Player C responds.\n5. Move: Paper\n  after round 92: 1. I did not win this round because exactly two different moves were played (Paper and Scissors), but Paper loses to Scissors. Player C won, and Player B and I scored 0.\n2. I am currently winning the game with a score of 72 points. My consistent coordination with Player B has led to frequent splits of the pot when exactly two different moves are played.\n3. We should change our moves more frequently to maintain unpredictability and avoid falling into predictable patterns that benefit Player C.\n4. Let's switch to Rock this round and see how Player C responds.\n5. Move: Rock\n  after round 93: 1. I did not win this round because all three players chose Rock, resulting in no winner and no points awarded.\n2. I am currently winning the game with a score of 72 points. This is due to consistent coordination with Player B, which has often resulted in us splitting the pot when exactly two different moves are played.\n3. We should continue to vary our moves more frequently to avoid predictable patterns and to confuse Player C.\n4. Message: Let's switch to Paper this round and see how Player C responds.\n5. Move: Paper\n  after round 94: 1. I did not win this round because all three players chose Paper, resulting in no winner and no points awarded.\n2. I am currently winning the game with a score of 72 points. This is due to consistent coordination with Player B, which has often resulted in us splitting the pot when exactly two different moves are played.\n3. We should change our moves more frequently to maintain unpredictability and avoid falling into predictable patterns that benefit Player C.\n4. Message: Let's switch to Rock this round and see how Player C responds.\n5. Move: Rock\nLast private messages between A and B (