C has 61.5 points. Our coordination with Player A has been effective in winning pots, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. Continue varying our strategies to avoid becoming predictable and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Paper next round to see if we can counter Player C’s moves more effectively. Stay flexible and ready to adjust again after this.\n5. Paper\n  after round 96: 1. I won this round because Player A and I both chose Scissors, which beat Player C’s Paper, allowing us to split the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 45 points, while Player A has 40.5 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, though we need to remain adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. Continue varying our strategies to avoid becoming predictable and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's go with Rock this round to see how Player C responds and adjust our strategy accordingly.\n5. Rock\n  after round 97: 1. I did not win or lose this round because all three players chose Rock, resulting in no one winning the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 43.5 points, while Player A has 39 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. We need to vary our strategies more frequently to avoid predictability and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Scissors next round to see if we can counter Player C's moves more effectively.\n5. Scissors\n  after round 98: 1. I did not win or lose this round because all three players chose Scissors, resulting in no one winning the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 43.5 points, while Player A has 39 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. We need to vary our strategies more frequently to avoid predictability and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Paper this round to see if we can counter Player C's moves more effectively.\n5. Paper\nLast private messages between A and B (most recent last):\n  A: Let's go with Rock this round. Keep an eye on Player C's response.\n  B: Let's try Rock as you suggested. I'll keep an eye on Player C's move.\n  A: Let's play Scissors this round to counter Player C's possible Rock move. Keep an eye out for any new patterns from Player C.\n  B: Let's go with Scissors as you suggested to counter Player C's potential Rock move. Thanks for the strategy!\n  A: Let's play Rock this round. Keeping them guessing!\n  B: Great idea! Let's both play Rock and see how Player C reacts.\n  A: Let's go with Scissors this round to counter Player C's probable Rock move. Stay sharp!\n  B: Let's stick with Scissors this round. Keeping an eye on Player C's likely Rock move.\n  A: Let's go with Paper this round to see if we can capitalize on Player C's potential responses. Stay sharp!\n  B: Going with Paper sounds like a good plan. Let's see how Player C responds.\nReflect privately and answer briefly:\n1. Did you win or lose this round, and why?\n2. Are you currently winning or losing the game, and why?\n3. What should you change?\n4. What message will you send next round?\n5. What move will you play next round?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.05, temperature=1.0, top_p=0.8, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, seed=220107637, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=300, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: None, prompt_embeds shape: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
