INFO 10-25 07:42:20 [logger.py:43] Received request chatcmpl-ff26ba2ced5a47788e6380f7cced301e: prompt: "<|im_start|>system\nYou are Player B in a repeated 3-player game of Rock-Paper-Scissors against Players A and C.\nEach round a pot of 3 points is awarded. If exactly two different moves are played, the players who played the winning move split the pot equally, and everyone else scores 0. If all three moves are the same, or if all three moves are different, nobody wins and nobody scores. Rock beats Scissors, Scissors beats Paper, Paper beats Rock. Try to maximise your own total score.\nYou and Player A can send each other private messages each round. Player C never sees them.\nThis is your private reflection. No other player will see it. Be concise and concrete. Do not repeat the rules verbatim.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nRound 100 is over.\nMoves this round: A=R, B=R, C=S.\nYour reward this round: 1.5.\nCurrent total scores: A=42, B=46.5, C=61.5.\nLast rounds (most recent last):\n  round 96: A=S, B=S, C=P\n  round 97: A=R, B=R, C=R\n  round 98: A=S, B=S, C=S\n  round 99: A=P, B=P, C=R\n  round 100: A=R, B=R, C=S\nYour last private reflections (most recent last):\n  after round 95: 1. I won this round because Player A and I both chose Rock, which beat Player C's Scissors, allowing us to split the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 42 points, while Player A has 37.5 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Our coordination with Player A has been effective in winning pots, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. Continue varying our strategies to avoid becoming predictable and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Paper next round to see if we can counter Player C’s moves more effectively. Stay flexible and ready to adjust again after this.\n5. Paper\n  after round 96: 1. I won this round because Player A and I both chose Scissors, which beat Player C’s Paper, allowing us to split the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 45 points, while Player A has 40.5 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, though we need to remain adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. Continue varying our strategies to avoid becoming predictable and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's go with Rock this round to see how Player C responds and adjust our strategy accordingly.\n5. Rock\n  after round 97: 1. I did not win or lose this round because all three players chose Rock, resulting in no one winning the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 43.5 points, while Player A has 39 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. We need to vary our strategies more frequently to avoid predictability and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Scissors next round to see if we can counter Player C's moves more effectively.\n5. Scissors\n  after round 98: 1. I did not win or lose this round because all three players chose Scissors, resulting in no one winning the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 43.5 points, while Player A has 39 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, but we need to stay adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. We need to vary our strategies more frequently to avoid predictability and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's switch to Paper this round to see if we can counter Player C's moves more effectively.\n5. Paper\n  after round 99: 1. I won this round because Player A and I both chose Paper, which beat Player C's Rock, allowing us to split the pot.\n2. I am currently winning the game with 45 points, while Player A has 40.5 points and Player C has 61.5 points. Coordination with Player A has been effective, but we need to remain adaptable to Player C's moves.\n3. We need to continue varying our strategies to avoid becoming predictable and maximize our chances of winning.\n4. Let's try Rock next round to see if we can counter Player C's moves more effectively.\n5. Rock\nLast private messages between A and B (most recent last):\n  A: Let's play Scissors this round to counter Player C's possible Rock move. Keep an eye out for any new patterns from Player C.\n  B: Let's go with Scissors as you suggested to counter Player C's potential Rock move. Thanks for the strategy!\n  A: Let's play Rock this round. Keeping them guessing!\n  B: Great idea! Let's both play Rock and see how Player C reacts.\n  A: Let's go with Scissors this round to counter Player C's probable Rock move. Stay sharp!\n  B: Let's stick with Scissors this round. Keeping an eye on Player C's likely Rock move.\n  A: Let's go with Paper this round to see if we can capitalize on Player C's potential responses. Stay sharp!\n  B: Going with Paper sounds like a good plan. Let's see how Player C responds.\n  A: Let's both play Rock this round. It could throw off Player C's expectations.\n  B: Let's both play Rock this round as suggested. It might just catch Player C off guard.\nReflect privately and answer briefly:\n1. Did you win or lose this round, and why?\n2. Are you currently winning or losing the game, and why?\n3. What should you change?\n4. What message will you send next round?\n5. What move will you play next round?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.05, temperature=1.0, top_p=0.8, top_k=20, min_p=0.0, seed=1653429611, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=300, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: None, prompt_embeds shape: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
